Subject: PHIL: Godel and Infinity (was Utility of Maths)

I congratulate Krzys' for his most interesting post:

> [..] every natural number is followed by its successor, but ... so
> what? Mycielski's system has that axiom, yet no infinity), when we
> introduce it, we must specify its nature (Continuum Hypothesis), and
> we must expect it to remain somewhat beyond our reach (Godel's
> Theorem), because we never experience infinity.

I'm somewhat surprised to find myself in agreement.  Today I was trying
to explain Godel's work to a friend who'd read _Godel, Escher & Bach_. 
He expressed scepticism because Godel's theorem only seemed to be making
statements about infinite things, which are hard to treat seriously as
concrete entities.  Do this all mean that if we remove the axiom of
infinity we no longer encounter undecidable theorems?  Is this
what's Mycielski's system is doing?

[On a related thread this friend has an almost exclusively symbolic-
linguistic mind.  He treats maths as rules for the manipulation of
symbols, and nothing else.  He never understood numbers until he
realised they were adverbs.  Adverbs?  Compare 'the three fish swam
away' with 'the brown fish swan away' and we see that numbers are just
a particular type of adverb.  So he regards maths as a subset of
linguistics.]

> Krzys'

