Eric S. Raymond writes:
> Fine.  This suggests that the set of theorems decidable in finite time is
> finite and sparse in the set of all WFFs.  But that's trivially obvious, and not
> at all the same as demonstrating that the set of WFFs decidable *in principle*
> (in your terms, if one had infinite time) is sparse (which would be the
> stronger result I was looking for).

  Indeed, if we consider Moravec's negative time-delay IC
(or his Time-Macine Turing-Machine [extropy#9]) then Chaitin's result
is trivial because such a machine could easily decide a theorem in
constant time.  There are lots of problems we encounter all the time
that are so hard that they may as well be undecidable
(e.g. like the traveling saleman problem on 10^30 cities). However,
they are still decidable in principle because given enough time and
enough space, we could solve them. I would be amazed
if the stronger result was proven.

  BTW, what is the significance of a 17,000 variable diophantine equation?
Because it's "sufficiently hard"? Has Chaitin has generalized his
result, because it seems even the weaker statement doesn't hold.
(e.g. the set of _all_ theorems decidable in finite time is finite and
sparse)

