From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: More on Chaitin

I agree with Eric Raymond and Robin Hanson that Chaitin's work doesn't
prove that maths is random.  These claims confuse the difference between
a theorem or problem being _very_ hard and being undecidable.  Saying
a computer can never solve a problem beyond a certain level of
complexity
(which Tim May has said) is a presently unverifiable claim about the
future, not an absolute truth.

There are analogies with chaos theory and the rhetorical claims made for
the incomputability of chaotic systems.  Chaos theory does not make the
prediction of weather impossible _in principle_, as is so often claimed
by authors like Paul Davies.  Just makes it incredibly more difficult. 
But infinity divided by any finite number is still infinity and to
philosophically blur the enormous with the infinite, as so many do, I
find inexpressibly frustrating.

As with chaos theory such sloppiness just kicks up dust and confuses
issues involved here.  This type of language always sets my bullshit
filters howling.  For instance, I interpret:

>  But as far as human reasoning goes, which of the two possibilities
>  is actually the case may as well be decided by flipping a coin; it
                        ^^^^^^^^^^^
>  is completely and forever undecidable, hence effectively random.
                                                ^^^^^^^^^^^

as:
   "which of the two possibilities is actually the case is a
    very computationally complex problem"

This is not to belittle Chaitin's work, nor the utility of Tim posting
the excerpts - they are very interesting and I'm impressed by the level
of debate on recent topics, and especially the recent maths threads. 
I'm learning a lot (which is one of the reasons why I subscribe, to
answer an earlier question Tim posed).

BTW, Tim, you mentioned Eric's use of dragon metaphors - are you sure
you're not thinking of my recent wormhole essay?

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

