Date: Tue, 18 May 93 02:08:48 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Final Words on Chaitin and Randomness

Math is random..."Is so!..."Is not!"..."Is so!"..."Is not!"

I seem to be outnumbered, though most (have any?) of the nay-sayers have
not read Chaitin's papers and seem to be focusing on semantic
hair-splitting about the meaning of the phrase "math is random." (One who
has actually located Chaitin's books, Derek Zahn, has not yet been heard
from.)

(My original remark was "The latest deepening of the crater was Chaitin's
1987 proof about the randomizness of mathematics at all levels (no, not the
mundane stuff about chaos, attractors, blah, blah)...Chaitin proved
arithmetic itself, the rock of mathematics, is itself fundamentally random!
As he put it, "God not only plays dice in quantum mechanics, but even with
the whole numbers."" And in a later post I made the point even more
clearly, citing both Chaitin's and Casti.)

I won't repeat the Casti quote, nor the quotes from Pagels or Davies (whom
Mike Price seems to dislike as a writer...). Here's one straigtht from
Chaitin:

"In conclusion, we see that proving whether particular exponential
diophantine equations have finitely or infinitely many solutions, is
absolutely intractable (Theorem D). Such questions escape the power of
mathematical reasoning. This is a region in which mathematical truth has no
discernable structure or pattern and appears to be completely random....

"I believe we have demonstrated in this book that randomness is already
present in pure mathematics, in fact, even in rather elementary branches of
number theory. This doesn't mean that the universe and mathematics are
lawless. it means that sometimes laws of different kind apply: statistical
laws."  [Chaitin, "Algorithmic Information Theory," 1987, p. 163]

(By the way, someone asked about what Diophantine equations have to do with
anything. Though this may indeed sound obscure, Chaitin merely used the
results of Jones and Matijasevec to "Godelize" programs for his abstract
machine. This form is much more compact than the notoriously complex Godel
numbering scheme used previously. Also, and not coincidentally, "any
formalized mathematical assertion can be encoded as a statement about
positive integers" (Chaitin, p. 7), thus allowing all mathematical
assertions to be analyzed in terms of solutions to Diophantine equations.
And the 17,000 -variable equation, which effectively is a kind of Turing
machine (using registers though). Chaitin will actually send you the tape
of LISP and REXX code used to run this abstract machine.)

Several points:

1. I obviously cannot recap Chaitin's entire proof, even if I understood it
well enough to condense it to fit for this list (a smart aleck might note
that Chaitin's proof is already incompressible and cannot be represented by
anything shorter than itself).

2. Some have criticized my citing of math writers like Casti, Pagels, and
Davies, on various stylistic grounds (e.g., Mike Price doesn't like the way
Davies had written about chaos and weather prediction...what this says
about whether Chaitin was correct in his arguments is unclear to me). I
cited these sources because a 2-4 page excerpt from one of Chaitin's papers
would of course convey even less information to the casual reader than
these writers have conveyed (because Chaitin is writing longer
pieces--proofs--and any given 2-4 page chunk is actually less complete than
a summary article or chapter. I chose the Casti citation because his was
the clearest. He also provided a historical context by citing the furor in
the mathematics community when this result was announced. (For example, one
of the reasons I went to the first A-Life Conference in September '87 was
to meet Chaitin, who was to talk on the impact of his results for
artificial life....he ended up cancelling out at the last minute--a major
disappointment for me.)

3. But the largest dispute here is over the *interpretation* of the  "math
is random" summary (bearing in mind what I actually said, the quotes from
sources, etc.).

Some sample reactions (I cannot quote the entire posts...these are
excerpts, and I don't thing they misrepresent the views of their authors):

Eric Raymond: "...'arithmetic is random' is a gross over-interpretation of it"

Ray Cromwell: " "Arithemetic is random" seems to be deliberately phrased to
cause controversy like Bennett's Quantum "teleportation."  It's not like
2+2 yields a random result."

Robin Hanson: "An infinity of undecideable facts is not at all the same as
saying
"arithmetic is random".  I agree that this summary is misleading."

Mike Price: "I agree with Eric Raymond and Robin Hanson that Chaitin's work
doesn't
prove that maths is random.  These claims confuse the difference between a
theorem or problem being _very_ hard and being undecidable.  Saying a
computer can never solve a problem beyond a certain level of complexity
(which Tim May has said) is a presently unverifiable claim about the
future, not an absolute truth."

(Before moving on, let me handle Mike's comment. There ARE problems which
are provably undecideable and hence absolutely intractable, as I have
asserted. Examples include Hilbert's Tenth Problem, and Berger's work on
domino tiling (which I have noted elsewhere). Cf. Garey and Johnson's
"Computers and Intractability: A Guide to the Theory of NP-Completeness."
Chaitin's work is not with merely "hard" problems, but with
undecideable/absolutely intractable problems. As near as we can tell, these
proofs about undecideability are much more than just "unverifiable claims
about the future." )

I would advise those who feel that "math is random" is misleading or wrong
to either more carefully analyze the real significance of Chaitin's
results, or to disprove Chaitin if you think he's wrong. (Not to argue from
"authority," but Chaitin's work is very well-regarded and has not been
disproved so far.)

Quibbling over confusing interpretations of "random" is pointless, when the
actual results are so compelling.

I'll be happy to discuss this further, provided folks have read enough
about these results to make the discussion fruitful.


-Tim May



