Jan Mycielski, "Analysis without actual infinity", Notices of the
American Mathematical Society 26(1979), A-523.
Jan Mycielski, "Analysis without actual infinity", Journal of
Symbolic Logic, but do not know know when exactly.
Jan Mycielski, "Finitistic real analysis", Real Analysis Exchange
vol. 6(1980-81), pp. 127-130.
address: Dr. Jan Mycielski, Professor of Mathematics, University
of Colorado, Boulder, CO 80309 (listed in the American Mathematical 
Society/Mathematical Association of America combined membership list).
Jas' (that's what prefers to be called, except by enemies) is
a quiet anarchocapitalist, but as I understand he does not use
computer mail.
Excellent source for review of the Axiom of Choice and Continuum
Hypothesis is: "Handbook of Mathematical Logic", edited by J. Barwise,
North-Holland, 1977.
Especially -- "About the Axiom of Choice" by Thomas J. Jech,
which includes the Banach-Tarski Paradox ("paradoxical" decomposition
of a ball) pp. 351-353, with a sketch of the proof,
and "Forcing" by John P. Burgess, including a discussion of the
Continuum Hypothesis and Godel's Theorem.

If I understand correctly Chaitin's result as presented by Eric,
a desirable result would be to place some natural topology on the
set of all theorems, and then somehow show that decidable theorems
form a set of first category, while the space itself is of the second
category. Or -- find a probability measure on the space and show
that probability measure of the set of decidable theorems is less
than one. But ... is the collection of decidable theorems a set?
Is the collection of all theorems a set? Anyways, Eric, if you do
something like that, you will get the Fields Prize and tenure at
Princeton.

It sure like a nice thing to work on. I would give it at least three, four
years. I have no idea yet how to even start on it.

Krzys'

