I too feel that Casti's rendition of Chaitin's work leaves something  
to be desired.  That said, I have a few comments that may get me into  
trouble:

>All right, so the digits of omega are random and the cardinality of  
>the "omega set" (the set of theorems mapped to the digits of omega)  
>is aleph-null.  That doesn't imply anything much about the
>*complement* of the omega set --- not even anything about its  
>cardinality!

For a set to be undecidable, it clearly must be infinite.  If it is  
undecidable, its complement must be.  Therefore, if a set is  
undecidable, its complement is infinite.  So, we do know this  
(trivial) thing about the cardinality of its complement. (I think  
that Eric knows this, but the sentence above seems to suggest  
otherwise.)

>This might be true, *if the omega set exhausted the space of all  
>theorems*. Unless I have misunderstood Costi's explanation, it does  
>not.

My impression is that omega covers the set of all Turing machines.   
It could just as easily cover the set of all finite formulas (i.e.,  
any countably infinite set).

Michael Clive Price asks:

>Do this all mean that if we remove the axiom of
>infinity we no longer encounter undecidable theorems? 


The question "Is this particular WFF a theorem?"  is always decidable  
(since the set of possibilities is finite). Decidablility is a  
property of sets.  For a set to be undecidable, it must be infinite.   
Perhaps you meant to ask "If there were only finite sets, would there  
be any *unprovable* theorems?".   


